# Benchmark forensics: preserved failed runs and per-seed logs are machine-local diagnosis.
# The scored results (bench/results/*.json) ARE tracked — they are the recall record.
# Covers *-failed-run/ too. Preservation was widened to EVERY seed (not only failures) so the
# envelopes carrying token accounting survive the worktree removal, and the new `<stamp>-<seed>-run`
# name silently escaped the old `*-failed-run/` pattern -- 2.8 MB of forensics one `git add -A`
# away from the history.
bench/results/*-run/
bench/results/*.log
# `--forge-only` output. It exists to be READ — it carries whole model-written paragraphs, the
# rationales the harness had regenerated to justify a moved anchor — so it is model text on the
# same footing as a node log, not a recall record. The `*.json` rule above would otherwise have put
# it on the tracked side purely because of its extension.
bench/results/*.forge.json
# `--rejudge` output. Same reason: an experiment on the JUDGE, run against reports that already
# exist, not a measurement of the audited node. It carries model-written citations and would be
# read as a recall record purely because of its extension.
bench/results/*.rejudge.json

# The suite's own TAP scratch file. run-all.mjs writes it while a run is in flight and removes it
# after; a killed or concurrent run leaves one behind, and one was nearly committed by an
# over-broad `git add`.
tests/.run-all-tap-*.txt
# Its per-file reporter's scratch file, the same way (telemetry-run-time.plan.md stage 3).
tests/.run-all-files-*.jsonl

# Run artifacts produced when quorate reviews itself.
.claude/reviews/*/evidence/
# Runs minted BY TESTS. They exercise the real mint path into the real reviews directory and clean
# up in a finally — but the suite runs every file twice concurrently (check-counted-facts spawns an
# inner `node --test`), so a cleanup can still be in flight when the tree is staged. Twice a fixture
# run reached a commit that way. tests/helpers.mjs mintSlug() gives them this prefix.
.claude/reviews/*-tmptest-*/

# Runs minted BY THE TEST SUITE, which must never enter the history of the repository they test.
# Three of them did (0dab5dd), along with a latest.txt pointing at one -- so the committed "most
# recent run" was a test fixture. The slug is a literal in the test; tests/regression-test-litter
# pins these two facts together so a renamed probe cannot quietly start being committed again.
.claude/reviews/*driftprobe*/
.claude/reviews/latest.txt
.claude/reviews/*/sandbox/*/declared-docs/
.claude/reviews/*/nodes/*.tmp
# The /request skill's cross-family critique. It is NOT a run — it writes before anything is minted,
# so `--evidence-dir` is "somewhere to put the reviewer's output" rather than a run directory, and the
# `*/evidence/` rule above therefore covers only half of it. The assembled prompt sat on the tracked
# side and a `git add -A` committed two of them: model-facing text that restates the request document
# verbatim, which is the same content class every other rule here keeps out. The document records the
# reviewer and model in its `critique:` field, which is the part worth keeping.
.claude/reviews/request-critique-*/
node_modules/
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Consumer-mount test sandbox: a cloned project quorate is installed into and reviewed.
# Never part of the framework; regenerated on demand.
.sandbox/
# claim-check drafts and checker results: model text, cited by path from tracked files (claim-check request)
.claude/claim-checks/
# decision panel records: the question, every seat's prompt and answer, the outcome; model text (autonomous-run.plan.md stage 5)
.claude/decisions/
# plan-review rounds: the brief, the envelope, the verdict and the round's telemetry record; model text, read by
# `scripts/telemetry.mjs report` (telemetry-records.plan.md stage 3)
.claude/plan-reviews/
# the suite's records: each test file's time and counts, this machine's runs only (telemetry-run-time.plan.md stage 3)
.claude/suite-runs/

# Session digests extracted from Claude Code transcripts (scripts/extract-sessions.mjs).
#
# RAW CONVERSATION AND ABSOLUTE PATHS. These hold the operator's own words verbatim, machine paths,
# and whatever a spawned node happened to print — the same content class as .claude/reviews/evidence,
# and excluded for the same reason. The CURATED page (blog/build-log.html) is what gets committed:
# quoting from a digest is a deliberate act with the operator's consent, not a side effect of
# running the extractor.
blog/sessions/

# The panel transport probe is a fact about ONE machine and one install: which reviewer CLIs
# are reachable here, with these credentials. It was tracked, and so shipped to every consumer
# mounting this submodule — who then read this machine's "both reachable" as their own and got
# no transport gap in not_checked[]. It also made every submodule pointer move abort for anyone
# who had probed, because their local probe modified a tracked file.
.claude/panel-check.json
# The tie-break transport probe, for exactly the same reason and after exactly the same
# mistake: it was tracked too, so a consumer inherited this machine's record of a path it had
# never exercised. Found one commit later, by this file being swept into that very commit.
.claude/tiebreak-check.json

# THE DEV INSTALL: a STABLE quorate, mounted where a consumer mounts it, so this repository can use
# its own skills while developing the engine. Three layers exist deliberately:
#
#   root/                          the engine being developed (dirty, possibly broken)
#   .claude/skills/quorate/        a PINNED checkout — what /request and /full-review resolve to here
#   .sandbox/<project>/.claude/skills/quorate/   the consumer mount, for validating the mount itself
#
# Ignored rather than tracked, for the reason the sandbox is: it is a checkout, not content. Tracking
# it would commit a second copy of the engine into the engine. And an UNIGNORED untracked copy is
# worse than either — mirrorWorkingTree copies untracked files into the arms' checkout, so a whole
# second engine would ride into the tree the external reviewers read.
#
# It is a `git worktree`, so it carries a real `.git` file and reports ITS OWN pinned commit rather
# than the enclosing repository's — the distinction CLAUDE.md records as costing a wrong conclusion
# ("a copied framework has no .git, so anything asking git for its version gets the ENCLOSING
# repository's commit"). Refresh it deliberately: see .claude/dev-install.json.
.claude/skills/

# THE DEV INSTALL'S LOCAL HALF. Both of these describe THIS machine, and the repository has paid for
# shipping that kind of file twice already — .claude/panel-check.json and .claude/tiebreak-check.json
# were both tracked once, so every consumer inherited one machine's record of a transport it had never
# exercised. Same rule here:
#
#   CLAUDE.local.md          carries `@.claude/skills/quorate/QUORATE.md`. Committed, that import would
#                            point every fresh clone at a path .claude/skills/ hides from it.
#   .claude/dev-install.json records which commit THIS machine pinned, and when. A clone has no dev
#                            install, so a tracked pin would describe an install that does not exist.
CLAUDE.local.md
.claude/dev-install.json
# Python caches the bytecode of an imported module beside it; the extractor tests import extractors/python/extract.py
# (G-9 of 20261020-094311-ciss-delivery, where one was committed).
__pycache__/

# The operator's identity patterns the publish scan looks for (scripts/publish.mjs): never tracked, since writing a
# username or an e-mail address into a tracked file is the leak the scan exists to stop.
.claude/publish.local.json
